{% import '_macros.j2' as m %}
{% set title = 'the ' ~ learner_name ~ ' for email targeting' %}
{% set datasets = [('hillstrom', 'Hillstrom'), ('sim', 'Simulator'), ('criteo', 'Criteo v2.1')] %}
{% set hs = 'targeting.hillstrom.' %}
{% set lost_on_hillstrom = raw(hs ~ learner ~ '.policy_value_test') < raw(hs ~ 'sure_things.policy_value_test') %}
{% set zero_in_hillstrom = raw(hs ~ learner ~ '.qini_lower') < 0 < raw(hs ~ learner ~ '.qini_upper') %}
{% set intended %}
The {{ learner_name }} ranks customers by how much a marketing contact changes their chance of
converting, so that a campaign can contact the customers the message moves and leave alone the ones
who would buy anyway, the ones who will not buy either way and the ones it puts off. With the policy
value curve it chooses how many to contact: the share where profit per thousand customers on the
list peaks. It is meant for one decision, whether to send one marketing contact to a customer, on
data from a randomized holdout it can learn from. It is not meant for any other decision about a
person, for Alderquist's customers before it has been fit and graded on an Alderquist holdout, for a
channel with no randomized holdout to learn from, or for pricing ad exposure on Criteo, which
publishes no costs.
{% endset %}
{% set data %}
Three datasets, each split by customer into training, validation and test shares of
{{ v('targeting.split.train') }}, {{ v('targeting.split.validation') }} and {{ v('targeting.split.test') }}
with one seeded draw per customer, so no customer sits in two splits.

- **Hillstrom**: {{ v(hs ~ 'treatment') }}, outcome {{ v(hs ~ 'outcome') }}; the women's arm is
  dropped. {{ v(hs ~ 'rows_train') }} customers to fit on, {{ v(hs ~ 'rows_validation') }} to choose on
  and {{ v(hs ~ 'rows_test') }} to report on. Features: recency, purchase history in dollars, whether
  the customer bought men's or women's merchandise, whether they are new, zip code class and purchase
  channel. {{ label('data.hillstrom.rows') }}
- **The simulator**: {{ v('sim.customers') }} simulated customers under a randomized email, where both
  potential outcomes are known ({{ v('targeting.sim.rows_train') }} to fit on,
  {{ v('targeting.sim.rows_validation') }} to choose on, {{ v('targeting.sim.rows_test') }} to report on).
  Features: recency, frequency, spend, tenure, category affinity, engagement, region and the share of
  visits on mobile. The true average effect is {{ v('sim.customers.ate_true') }}, and
  {{ v('sim.customers.negative_effect_share') }} of customers have a true effect below zero: the email
  makes them less likely to buy. {{ label('sim.customers') }}
- **Criteo**: the full v2.1 release, {{ v('data.criteo.rows') }} rows with a treated share of
  {{ v('data.criteo.treated_share') }}, outcome {{ v('targeting.criteo.outcome') }}. The learners are fit
  on a seeded {{ v('targeting.criteo.rows_train_used') }} of the {{ v('targeting.criteo.rows_train') }}
  training rows; the {{ v('targeting.criteo.rows_validation') }} validation and
  {{ v('targeting.criteo.rows_test') }} test rows are scored whole. Features: the twelve anonymized fields
  the release carries. {{ label('data.criteo.rows') }}
{% endset %}
{% set fit %}
{{ method|trim }}

Every model is LightGBM, single threaded and seeded, with small trees:
{{ v('targeting.learner.n_estimators') }} rounds at a learning rate of {{ v('targeting.learner.learning_rate') }},
{{ v('targeting.learner.num_leaves') }} leaves and at least {{ v('targeting.learner.min_child_samples') }}
customers per leaf. The settings are modest on purpose: the outcomes are rare and the effects are a
point or two of conversion, so deeper trees fit noise in each arm (`marginal_targeting.learners`).

The protocol is the same on every dataset (`marginal_targeting.protocol`): fit on the training split
alone; on the validation split, choose the share to contact where profit per thousand customers on
the list peaks, on a grid from {{ v('targeting.share_grid.first') }} to {{ v('targeting.share_grid.last') }}
in steps of {{ v('targeting.share_grid.step') }}; report every published figure from the test split,
scored once. Qini intervals come from a paired bootstrap of {{ v('targeting.bootstrap_replicates') }}
replicates at {{ v('targeting.level') }}, every policy scored on the same resampled customers. The
chosen share must pass the interior test: strictly inside the grid, with the curve lower on both
sides, or the page calls the operating point degenerate. Profit on Hillstrom is the spend the email
causes at a {{ v('targeting.hillstrom.margin_on_spend') }} margin less {{ v('targeting.hillstrom.cost_per_contact') }}
per email; on the simulator, {{ v('targeting.sim.revenue_per_conversion') }} per conversion at a
{{ v('targeting.sim.margin') }} margin less {{ v('targeting.sim.cost_per_contact') }}; on Criteo, incremental
visits with no cost. The Criteo run took {{ v('targeting.criteo.seconds') }} seconds against a budget of
{{ v('targeting.criteo.budget_seconds') }}.
{% endset %}
