{% endset %}
{% set contract %}
{% call m.line('trained', 'met') %}
Fit in the pipeline (`make targeting`) on each dataset's training split ({{ v('targeting.split.train') }} of customers, split by customer with one seeded draw), against two baselines that are not straw men: the sure things rule and emailing everyone.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('timed', 'met') %}
The features describe each customer before the contact and the outcome follows it (on Hillstrom, {{ v(hs ~ 'outcome') }}); the share is chosen on validation before the test split is scored, once, and the manifest is as of {{ manifest.as_of }}.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('calibrated', 'partly') %}
Its scores are rankings, not calibrated probabilities; the Qini coefficient carries a paired bootstrap interval at {{ v('targeting.level') }} on each test split, and on the simulator the predicted uplift is checked against the true effect (PEHE {{ v('targeting.sim.pehe.' ~ learner) }}).
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('useful', 'met') %}
It picks who gets the email: the share contacted is chosen where profit peaks on validation, at {{ v('targeting.hillstrom.cost_per_contact') }} per email and a {{ v('targeting.hillstrom.margin_on_spend') }} margin on the spend it causes on Hillstrom; Criteo carries no cost, so there it only ranks.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('fair', 'not built') %}
None of the features is a protected attribute, but zip code class on Hillstrom and region on the simulator can stand in for one, and the build did not compare who is contacted across them.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('gated', 'partly') %}
The protocol refuses to choose a share on the test or the training split and refuses an outcome that is not binary, and the interior test marks an operating point on the edge of the grid as degenerate; no bound on the fit's quality fails it.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('served', 'not served') %}
The API has no targeting route; the scores live in `results/targeting` and the site's marts.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('integrated', 'met') %}
The site's Targeting page reads its Qini and policy value curves and the quadrant table from the marts, and the quarterly review quotes its test split figures.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('monitored', 'not built') %}
There is no scheduled refit and no check on how its ranking holds up over time; the run's steps are logged in `logs/targeting.log`.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('documented', 'met') %}
This card and `docs/evaluation.md`, rendered from the manifest by the claim gate.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('bounded', 'met') %}
The statement closes this card: Hillstrom and Criteo are public research releases and the simulated customers are generated, so no real customer of Alderquist is scored.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('validated', 'met') %}
Graded on randomized holdouts, the Hillstrom and Criteo test splits scored once, and on the simulator's known effects, never on the data it was fit on.
{% endcall %}
{% call m.line('explainable at the decision', 'met') %}
A reader sees the Qini curve with its bootstrap band, the policy value curve by share with the chosen share and its interior test, the top decile uplift, and on the simulator which quadrants the list reaches.
{% endcall %}
{% endset %}
{% set limitations %}
{% if lost_on_hillstrom or zero_in_hillstrom %}
On the one real randomized test it has not shown it can rank: on Hillstrom it
{{ 'earned less than the sure things rule' if lost_on_hillstrom else 'did not separate from the sure things rule' }}{% if zero_in_hillstrom %} and its Qini interval includes zero{% endif %}.
The evidence that it works comes from the simulator, where the effects were written to be found.

{% endif %}
Hillstrom's features are few and coarse, and its effects are a point or less of conversion, so a
test split of {{ v(hs ~ 'rows_test') }} customers carries more noise than the differences between
policies: emailing everyone, which ranks no one, earns {{ v(hs ~ 'everyone.policy_value_validation') }}
on validation and {{ v(hs ~ 'everyone.policy_value_test') }} on test.

On Criteo the chosen share is {{ v('targeting.criteo.' ~ learner ~ '.chosen_share') }}, a degenerate
operating point, because the release carries no cost per contact; the ranking is tested there, the
decision is not.

{{ weakness|trim }}

The build did not check who it contacts across zip code classes or regions, and it is not served.
{% endset %}
